<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Metohija</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dukagjin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Metohija is the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, a fertile basin drained by the White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drin</a> that includes the towns of Peć, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjakova">Gjakova</a>. Its name comes from the Greek word for church estates, recalling the lands of medieval Serbian monasteries such as the Patriarchate of Peć. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> call the region Dukagjin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/metohija.jpg" alt="Metohija" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_geographical_regions_of_Kosovo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Metohija covers 35 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, 3,891 square kilometres, and is about 23 kilometres wide at its broadest and about 60 kilometres long, at an average altitude of 450 metres. The Mokra Gora, the Accursed Mountains, Pashtrik, the Šar Mountains and Drenica enclose it. It takes in the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjakova">Gjakova</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pec">Peja</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a>. Its fertile land is watered by many small rivers and has a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> influence; it is known for vineyards, fruit orchards and chestnut and almond trees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the Greek metochia, 'monastic estates', a reference to the many villages and estates owned by Serbian Orthodox monasteries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The Albanian name Rrafshi i Dukagjinit takes its name from the Dukagjini family, who ruled much of the area in the 14th and 15th centuries. Jahja Drançolli argues that Metohija did not appear as a geographic term in the medieval and Ottoman periods, only for ecclesiastical property, and that Serbian scholars first used it so in the 19th and 20th centuries.</p><p>After the Roman conquests the area was divided between Dardania and Praevalitana. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in the 6th and 7th centuries, and in 1019 the region became part of the Eparchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a>. Stefan Nemanja was recognised as independent in 1190 and kept the north, Hvosno, while the south joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> by the early 13th century. After 1371 the Balšić family and from 1378 the Branković family controlled it, and it remained in the Serbian Despotate until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> conquered it in 1455.</p><p>Ottoman registers of the 16th century suggest that the plains had a majority Albanian population, mostly Christian farmers. In 1912 the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> took most of the region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> area. In 1944 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Yugoslav Partisans</a> liberated it from occupation, and in 1946 it became part of Serbia's Autonomous Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and Metohija.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 570,147 people in Metohija. The term 'Metohija' was official in the names of the Autonomous Region and Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and Metohija from 1945 to 1968, was dropped in 1968, and was restored by the Serbian constitution of September 1990. On 17 February 2008 representatives of Kosovo Albanians declared independence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> continued to treat Metohija as part of its territory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Metohija” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The federation joined the Republic of Serbia, with its autonomous provinces of Vojvodina and Kosovo and <b>Metohija</b>, and the Republic of Montenegro under a cooperative federalism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the First Balkan War Montenegro besieged and took Scutari in 1913, but the powers forced it to hand the town to the new Albania; it did gain much of the Sandžak and <b>Metohija</b>, including Peć and Đakovica.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-montenegro">Kingdom of Montenegro</a></li><li><blockquote>Serbia does not recognise it and claims it as its Autonomous Province of Kosovo and <b>Metohija</b>, although it accepts the governing authority of Kosovo's institutions under the 2013 Brussels Agreement.</blockquote><a href="/state/kosovo">Kosovo</a></li></ul></section>
